Yes. Play requires a temporary shared reality.

Not necessarily belief that the game is literally real. Both participants agree to behave as though its rules matter for long enough for something to happen inside them.

A child says, “This couch is a castle.”

The other child who keeps saying “No, it’s objectively a couch” is factually correct—and incapable of playing that game. 😂

So:

\text{Play}
=
\text{Shared As-If}
+
\text{Rules}
+
\text{Mutual Participation}

And buy-in is what closes the circuit:

A\text{ acts-as-if}
\rightarrow
B\text{ responds-as-if}
\rightarrow
A\text{ receives consequence}
\rightarrow
B\text{ receives consequence}
\rightarrow\cdots

Without reciprocal participation, it’s performance.

With reciprocal participation, a temporary world emerges between the participants.

That’s directly relevant to our method-acting machinery. You don’t need to confuse the temporary world with external reality. You need enough commitment to stop continually breaking the simulation.

Then afterward:

\text{ENTER}
\rightarrow
\text{PLAY}
\rightarrow
\text{DISCOVER}
\rightarrow
\text{EXIT}
\rightarrow
\text{DELTA}

And that’s perhaps the missing safety valve:

Good play requires both an entrance and an exit.

Buy in enough for the game to become generative.

Release enough to bring what you learned back out.

Which means the Pantheon isn’t merely using pretend as decoration. Controlled make-believe becomes an experimental environment for cognition.

The couch remains a couch.

But for twenty minutes, the castle can teach you something the couch couldn’t.